+## 2026-08-03 — #898, a run that drops API calls cannot look clean (PR 3, closes the cluster)
+
+- (CPO ruling, recorded when the batch was taken before PR 1, implemented here) THE THRESHOLD POLICY
+  IS **VISIBLE ALWAYS, FAIL ONLY ON STAGNATION**. Dropped calls are counted by endpoint and shown in
+  the job summary on EVERY run. A single bad run never fails, because failing blocks the dbt build
+  and daily freshness is a stated non-negotiable. The run fails only when the SAME endpoint drops on
+  consecutive runs, i.e. when it is demonstrably not healing. It reuses the existing
+  `stagnant_statistics` no-progress-since-last-run pattern and its existing snapshot table, which
+  was the explicit instruction: do not invent a mechanism.
+
+- (N settled by rule, not escalated) N = 2 consecutive runs. `detect_stagnant_statistics_backfill`
+  already compares exactly prior-versus-current, and both gates now share ONE exit path, so a second
+  stagnation signal on that path uses the same window. Two gates on one exit code disagreeing about
+  what "stagnant" means would be a defect, not a choice. Per the standing rule, a question a rule
+  can settle is not put to the CPO.
+
+- (⚠ THE FRESHNESS CONSEQUENCE, traced in the workflow rather than assumed) A hard fail returns 503,
+  which `main.py` maps to exit code 3. In `dbt-scheduled.yml` every post-ingest step is conditioned
+  on `steps.ingest.outputs.new_data == 'true'` with **no `always()`**, and GitHub Actions ANDs an
+  implicit `success()` into that. So a nonzero ingest exit SKIPS `dbt deps`, `dbt seed`, the
+  layer-contract check and `dbt build`: on the day the gate fires, the marts are a day stale. This
+  is not new behaviour introduced here (the existing `stagnant_statistics` gate already exits the
+  same way); this diff adds a second trigger to the same path. Stated explicitly because it trades
+  daily freshness, which is CPO territory, and the CPO approved the trade knowing a fail blocks the
+  build.
+
+- (the retry is BEST EFFORT and is documented as such rather than oversold) `fetch_json` now gives
+  the per-minute limit the single retry a 429 already got, because it IS a 429 that the provider
+  chose to deliver as HTTP 200 with the error in the body. That is why `raise_for_status()` passed
+  and the call was dropped with ZERO retries. But a per-minute window can take up to 60s to clear
+  and the retry waits `Retry-After` or 3s, so it will not rescue every call. Waiting out a full
+  minute per drop was considered and rejected: it trades run time for a case #897's pacing should
+  already have made rare. The guarantees live elsewhere — #897 makes limits rare, #896 makes a drop
+  non-destructive, and this makes what remains impossible to miss.
+
+- (the ordering bug avoided) The prior dropped-call counts are read BEFORE this run's are persisted.
+  Reading after would compare the run against itself and every run would look stagnant. The read and
+  the write both use the existing `COMPLETENESS_SNAPSHOT_TABLE`.
+
+- (⭐ THE #896 LESSON APPLIED, and it is why this contract's impact_map looks different) #896 round 1
+  failed because the impact map traced the one table the task was about while the diff edited a
+  helper shared by seven callers. This task edits TWO shared functions, so both were enumerated
+  before writing a line:
+  - `append_api_errors` has **16 call sites** (`grep -rn "append_api_errors(" ingestion/`), and every
+    one passes a context whose FIRST token is the endpoint name. That is what makes counting by
+    endpoint possible without touching a single call site, and the change adds a counter increment
+    only: the return value, the messages appended to the sink, and their order are untouched.
+  - `fetch_json` is the single HTTP entry point for every endpoint. The new branch adds no key to
+    the returned dict, which is the constraint #896 established, and #896's
+    `TestReturnedKeySetIsStable` would catch a regression.
+  - The snapshot table gains a key, so its consumers were checked rather than assumed:
+    `grep -rn "INGEST_COMPLETENESS_SNAPSHOT" dbt_project/ scripts/` returns NOTHING and no dbt
+    source or model references it. It is internal run state, so the extra key has zero downstream
+    blast radius. That is precisely the check whose absence caused #896 round 1.
+
+- (the dangerous flag, guarded by a test) `_payload_shows_minute_rate_limit` must NEVER set
+  `_http_quota_exhausted`. That flag stops all remaining HTTP for the run, which is right for a
+  daily cap that will not recover and catastrophic for a limit that clears within the minute. There
+  is a test asserting the detector leaves it false, and another asserting the two detectors do not
+  overlap in either direction.
+
+- (verification) 19 new tests, full suite 547 passed. Confirmed they pin: disabling the retry branch
+  fails `test_body_level_limit_is_retried_once` with "a per-minute limit must be retried" and
